                "text": "To solve  \n\\[\n\\sin(x) + x^2 = 42,\n\\]\nrewrite it as finding zeros of\n\\[\nf(x)=x^2+\\sin(x)-42.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(-1 \\le \\sin(x) \\le 1\\), we must have\n\\[\n41 \\le x^2 \\le 43 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x \\approx \\pm \\sqrt{42}\\approx \\pm 6.48,\n\\]\nso we expect two real solutions (one positive, one negative). Using a numerical root-finder on \\(f(x)\\), the solutions are:\n\n\\[\nx \\approx 6.4666500409\n\\]\n\\[\nx \\approx -6.4970982170\n\\]\n\nSo the equation has **two real roots**: \\(\\boxed{x \\approx 6.46665}\\) and \\(\\boxed{x \\approx -6.49710}\\).",
